<div class="<%= layout_framework_classes %>">
  <div class="<%= layout_framework_title_classes %>">
    <%= render 'framework/page_header', page_name: :themes %>
  </div>

  <div class="<%= layout_framework_content_classes %>">
    <%= render_framework_intro_paragraph(:themes) %>

    <% pinned_semver = docs_released_plugins_urls&.dig(:semver) %>
    <% if pinned_semver && !Framework.public_root.join("css/#{pinned_semver}/themes").exist? %>
      <div class="<%= message_sage_classes %>">
        <div class="flex items-center w-full">
          <div class="flex-shrink-0 items-center">
            <%= render 'shared/icons/info' %>
          </div>
          <div class="w-full flex items-center justify-between ml-3">
            <p class="text-sm font-medium">
              <strong>Pinned release notice:</strong> this docs version renders against framework
              <%= pinned_semver %>, which predates themes. The demos below and the picker's Style
              selector have no visible effect on it. A local docs server serves the live build on the
              current docs version, where the demos render.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <div class="space-y-16">
      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Usage') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Each theme ships as a standalone stylesheet alongside <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">plugins.css</code>.
            Include the theme file and add <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--theme-&lt;id&gt;</code> to your screen element.
            The theme picks the colors; the device still decides how they print: dither patterns
            on 1-bit, solids on full color.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            A theme already decides every color, so
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--dark-mode</code> has no effect on a themed screen.
            A theme that wants its own dark variant can style
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">.screen--theme-&lt;id&gt;.screen--dark-mode</code> in its stylesheet.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Monochrome plugin icons follow the theme automatically when they carry the
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">image--adaptive</code> class. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :image) %>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: '<link rel="stylesheet" href="plugins.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="themes/black-and-yellow-theme.css">

<div class="screen screen--theme-black-and-yellow">...</div>'
          ) %>
        </div>

        <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          To preview any docs example with a theme, pick a Style in the screen picker. It applies to
          every example on the page and persists across pages.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Available Themes') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The framework ships these themes. Apply one in your own markup with its
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--theme-&lt;id&gt;</code> class:
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
            <% Framework::Themes::NAMES_BY_ID.each do |theme_id, theme_name| %>
              <li><strong><%= theme_name %></strong>: <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--theme-<%= theme_id %></code></li>
            <% end %>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Dark is how you render a plugin dark. Add
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--theme-dark</code> to the screen element
            and every surface recolors; it is a theme like any other.
            The plugin's own markup stays as written.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            To see them in action, open the screen picker (top right) and choose a Style. The
            example below has no theme of its own, so it follows the device and the Style you
            pick, exactly as your plugin would on a themed screen.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="<%= framework_example_classes %>">
          <div class="<%= framework_example_inner_classes %>">
            <div class="<%= framework_example_wrapper_classes %>">
              <div class="screen">
                <div class="view view--full">
                  <div class="layout layout--col gap--space-between">
                    <div class="grid">
                      <div class="col--span-2">
                        <div class="grid grid--cols-1">
                          <div class="item">
                            <div class="meta"></div>
                            <div class="content">
                              <span class="value value--small">43</span>
                              <span class="label">Sales Calls</span>
                            </div>
                          </div>
                          <div class="item">
                            <div class="meta"></div>
                            <div class="content">
                              <span class="value value--small">7</span>
                              <span class="label">New Clients</span>
                            </div>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="item col--span-4">
                        <div class="meta"></div>
                        <div class="content">
                          <span class="value value--xxlarge value--tnums" data-fit-value="true">4,283</span>
                          <span class="label">Reams Sold</span>
                        </div>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="divider"></div>
                    <div class="progress-bar">
                      <div class="content">
                        <span class="label">Q4 Paper Quota</span>
                        <span class="label">60%</span>
                      </div>
                      <div class="track">
                        <div class="fill" style="width: 60%"></div>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <div class="title_bar">
                    <%= image_tag plugin_image_path("trmnl--render.svg"), class: "image image--adaptive", alt: "TRMNL Logo" %>
                    <span class="title">Dunder Mifflin</span>
                    <span class="instance">Sales</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Themes in JavaScript') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Themes never ship JavaScript. A theme is only CSS, and JavaScript can read everything
            it needs from that CSS:
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint</code>: reads every color and pattern
              from the CSS itself, so charts and other JS-drawn visuals follow the active theme.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.cssVar()</code>: reads any
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--*</code> variable a theme sets on the
              screen. A theme that wants to hand its own values to plugin code just declares them;
              no framework changes needed.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_api) %>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'The Themes API') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            A theme is written against a fixed set of surfaces: it picks their colors, and rendering
            stays the framework's job. The two pages below cover the workflow and the full list of
            surfaces.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
          <%= render FrameworkDocCardComponent.new(icon: :edit, title: 'Authoring Themes', docs_page: :theme_authoring) do %>
            <p class="<%= framework_subsection_description_classes %>">The contract and workflow: start from the boilerplate, map slots, register the id, and lint.</p>
          <% end %>

          <%= render FrameworkDocCardComponent.new(icon: :bounding_box, title: 'Theme Slots', docs_page: :theme_slots) do %>
            <p class="<%= framework_subsection_description_classes %>">Every surface a theme can recolor, from semantic colors and components to utilities, borders, and chart series.</p>
          <% end %>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <%= render 'framework/page_navigation' %>
  </div>
</div>
